--- a/runtime/platform/README.md
+++ b/runtime/platform/README.md
@@ -2,9 +2,21 @@
 
 | File | What it is |
 |---|---|
+| `ps5_game_slice.c`, `ps5_game_slice.h` | Executes two actual main functions generated privately from the local 1.0.0 dump: verifies main hash, runs vector transforms through checked memory and supplies frame-by-frame results to a visualization. No init_array, nnMain or gameplay startup |
+| `ps5_guest_memory.c`, `ps5_guest_memory.h` | Registry owning up to 64 eager mappings or sparse reservations, shared by checked CPU/HLE copies: 4 KiB commit/decommit/protection, dirty pages, privileged copies and synchronized unmap. Writes preflight all backing across mappings before changing bytes; backing failure poisons the failed map. No aliases or GPU coherence |
+| `ps5_mem.c`, `ps5_mem.h` | Runtime memory interface over sparse checked reservations, plus host-only demand allocations for sealed dispatch tables. Lazy/watch/fault callbacks run outside registry locks. Native compile and host-model checks only; full CPU/HLE binding, raw pointer conversion and GPU coherence remain pending. Not a transparent fault pager for guest pointers |
+| `ps5_cpu_memory.c` | Process-wide scalar/vector/atomic hooks over runtime memory, with lazy/fault/watch handling and explicit fatal errors. No scoped diagnostic recovery or thread-local guest space. Select with `ps5.py --runtime-memory`, mutually exclusive with probe hooks; native compile/host checks do not prove full-game startup or HLE integration |
+| `ps5_cpu_probe.c`, `ps5_cpu_probe.h` | Original synthetic AArch64 routines translated offline by the project lifter, run with opt-in scalar/vector checked copies and serialized checked atomics and scoped diagnostic fault recovery. Validates values, 4 KiB boundaries and contended RMW; no game execution or general memory backend |
 | `platform.h` | The operating system interface the rest of the runtime uses |
 | `linux.c`, `linux_int.h` | Linux: threads, preemption through signals, fibers; the header is what the `linux_*.c` parts share |
 | `linux_sync.c` | Linux: clock, locks and condition variables on futex |
+| `ps5_sync.c` | PS5 monotonic time, inline reader-writer locks and condition variables with bounded pthread parking; exercised paths passed the user's firmware-9 MKWPROBE-001 run |
+| `ps5_int.h`, `ps5_stack.c` | Internal guarded demand-stack allocation and accounting: 64 KiB startup backing, remaining units backed on touch, alternate signal stacks retained through pthread destructors; demand growth/migration passed MKWPROBE-001, destructor stress is a host check |
+| `ps5_threads.c` | PS5 thread creation with explicit stacks and startup handshake, join and timed completion, identity, yield, and a reaper that reclaims detached stacks and retired signal stacks after the pthread has ended; CPU topology, affinity and priorities remain pending |
+| `ps5_fibers.c` | x86-64 stack/context switching, per-thread root context and fiber migration; inherits the existing Linux switch's register and floating-point frame, with PS5 stack allocation |
+| `ps5_intr.c` | Scheduler interruption over reserved SIGUSR2, guarded alternate stack and SDK PS5 ucontext layout; suspension/register snapshot/resumption passed MKWPROBE-001; PC redirection remains host-tested only |
+| `ps5_nso_link.c`, `ps5_nso_link.h` | Diagnostic guest-only MOD0/symbol/RELA/packed RELR linking through checked copies; validates every destination before writing and compares complete linked-image hashes; no HLE installation or execution |
+| `ps5_nso_probe.c`, `ps5_nso_probe.h` | Diagnostic-only raw NSO loading at fixed layout addresses: compressed segments, file/image SHA-256, BSS, and RO/RW tests through the SDK's checked-copy API. Does not bind imports, relocate, instrument direct guest accesses or execute game code |
 | `linux_fs.c` | Linux: files and file system (atomic write, rename without replace) |
 | `linux_mem.c` | Linux: virtual memory, write watching with userfaultfd (Linux 6.7+), fault handler; `plat_mem_poke` writes through the protection via `/proc/self/mem` |
 | `linux_sys.c` | Linux: process, desktop, libraries, machine load (CPU, temperature, GPU) and desktop shortcut |
@@ -15,3 +27,33 @@
 | `win32_load.c` | Windows: machine load (CPU, PDH for temperature and GPU) and desktop shortcut |
 | `common.c`, `fp_x86.h` | Common parts, x86 floating point control |
 | `mkw.rc`, `mkw.manifest` | `mkw.exe`'s manifest (UTF-8 code page, from totk-pc-recomp); `build.py --target windows` |
+
+The direct PS5 title also uses `ps5_fs.c` (the upstream POSIX file implementation
+adapted to /app0 and the SDK directory bindings), `ps5_sys.c` (native process/time,
+hardware entropy, crash reporting and explicit unsupported desktop facilities),
+`ps5_title.h` (the title pad bridge) and `ps5_hle_copy.c`/`ps5_hle_copy.h` (checked
+SDK bulk copies). Native HLE startup pre-backs the core's lazy commit chunks;
+it does not reserve physical backing for the entire heap. Raw typed HLE pointer
+accesses still have native-page granularity on mixed pages; this is an
+experimental boot build, not proof of complete 4 KiB HLE enforcement or GPU coherence.
+
+The native crash reporter explicitly binds to the SDK demand dispatcher and
+services demand reads/writes before reporting fatal exceptions. This permits
+dispatch-table and fiber allocation after crash-hook setup without replacing
+an unrelated signal handler; execute and sealed-write faults remain fatal.
+
+PS5 enumeration resolves entry metadata by full path and propagates readdir/stat failures instead of silently reporting an empty directory. Loose RomFS can use a PC-generated metadata index to avoid title directory enumeration.
+
+Thread-start failures identify allocation, demand-stack creation, pthread attributes/create or alternate signal-stack attachment, retaining pthread return codes and errno through cleanup.
+
+Stack initialization uses SDK owner-side prepare. Backing phase and raw kernel result are written to the runtime log before cleanup, including addresses/physical offset.
+
+PS5 plat_mem_commit normalizes byte ranges to touched logical 4 KiB pages, including overflow checks. The strict SDK API and adjacent logical permissions are preserved; this is not rounding permissions to native 16 KiB pages.
+
+PS5 module lookup uses relocated linker bounds and never reads ELF headers from execute-only text.
+
+PS5 HLE memcmp compares bounded private snapshots after range validation, preserving the SDK unsigned-byte difference and sparse-zero semantics.
+
+The checked registry protects mapping lifetime with a reader/writer gate. Single-region copies use the shared gate and the SDK region lock; multi-region copies (including reads), atomics, privileged protection-changing copies, unmap and protection changes use exclusive whole-transaction ownership.
+
+Ready scalar/vector CPU accesses try the checked transaction directly. Rejection enters the existing lazy/watch/fault path before retrying. Single-region copies avoid staging and duplicate rights/backing checks; the SDK still checks every logical page before touching bytes and records dirty writes.
